{"type":"thinking","text":"Synthesis closeout requires registry findings to reflect iteration findings. This is a critical detail: my iteration delta `finding` rows must be picked up by reduce-state into `findings-registry.json` `keyFindings`. Then synthesis-closeout will validate. Good — we run reduce-state before synthesis, so registry should contain them.\n\nIt checks `missingArtifacts` maybe includes research.md, dashboard, config? Let's look at the evidence collection earlier to know if research.md must exist before closeout (yes it is output). We'll create research.md before running closeout.\n\nAlso `synthesis_complete` event staged and then gatewayed. The gateway stem registry supports `deep_research.synthesis_complete`? Pinned legacy events include synthesis_complete? The `PINNED_LEGACY_EVENTS` includes 'synthesis_complete' (but that's for pinning legacy rows). The closeout stages canonical `stem` events, so fine.\n\nNow check `reduce-state.cjs` requirements: it may require running from repo root and reads state log. It might fail if no dashboard provenance etc. We'll see.\n\nAlso the state log after init: step_create_state_log event `deep_research.run_initialized` with scope runId=sessionId, lineageId=sessionId. The gateway's research adapter likely requires an authority record? Let's just try.\n\nOne more thing: config `specFolder` — dispatch says spec_folder: specs/...019-dsh-repin-rc3. The artifact dir is the lineage. Config's specFolder should be the target spec (not lineage). The reducer invocation uses spec_folder plus --artifact-dir. Good.\n\n`resource_map_present`: check if `{spec_folder}/resource-map.md` exists. It likely doesn't. We'll set false. `resource_map.emit`: true. If false, synthesis skips resource map. What should we use? Default true per command. It doesn't matter much; setting true may cause reducer to write `resource-map.md` inside artifact dir (allowed). Actually step_emit_resource_map in fan-out path runs `reduce-state.cjs {spec_folder} --emit-resource-map --fanout-resource-map-only` without --artifact-dir. That could resolve the artifact root to the spec folder's research/ — outside our lineage! Danger: it might write outside lineage. The dispatch says do NOT write outside. So I must either set `resource_map.emit: false` to skip it, or run the step with `--artifact-dir` (but that deviates). Better: set `config.resource_map.emit` false. Is that acceptable? It's a config field and a legitimate option (`--no-resource-map`). The research topic doesn't need a resource map. Setting false avoids an out-of-scope write. Record this in the iteration/synthesis as a workflow deviation within write limits.\n\nActually wait: for fan-out lineage, `step_emit_resource_map` branch_on config.fanout. Does our lineage config have `fanout`? We're a lineage, not the parent. The config likely doesn't have a `fanout` key. Then branch if_absent runs `reduce-state.cjs {spec_folder} --emit-resource-map`, which resolves artifact root: it might find multiple lineage packets? Uncertain. Could write `{spec_folder}/research/...`. Avoid entirely with emit false. Good.\n\nAlso `step_writeback_spec_findings` writes to spec.md outside lineage — skip, record as scope violation/deviation. `step_validate_post_synthesis_spec_doc` would be out of scope and unnecessary — skip.\n`step_update_config_status`: config is inside lineage → allowed.\n`step_telemetry_heartbeat_*`: writes `{state_paths.status_ledger}` = lineage/observability? Actually status_ledger = {artifact_dir}/orchestration-status.log, inside lineage, allowed. But the YAML's telemetry steps are skipped when config.fanout present; our config doesn't have fanout (lineage config). Running them writes orchestration-status.log inside lineage; fine but extra. It uses tsx import; may work. Not essential. I could skip telemetry steps since this is a fan-out lineage (the base has its own status log). Actually the run's `skip_when: config.fanout is present` means for single-executor runs. For lineage runs, fanout-run passes effective config? Hmm. The real fanout-run spawns a CLI with prompt; the CLI runs phase_init as single-executor path? Wait, read the prompt: \"The workflow YAML's per-iteration executor-dispatch steps are ALREADY SATISFIED by this process\". It also says run phase_init, main loop, synthesis. The fanout lineage config is its own. I think skipping telemetry is acceptable and safer (avoid tsx dependency issues). Not required for the lineage's outputs. I'll skip telemetry steps and note it.\n\nActually, the dispatch prompt says: \"State records go through the append gateway instead: hand every one, from the run-opening config row (step_create_state_log) to the terminal synthesis record, to append-mode-event.cjs exactly as the workflow YAML runs it.\" Terminal synthesis record = synthesis_complete via closeout. So we must do the closeout + gateway. Good.\n\nWhat about `step_fanout_merge`? That's in the parent synthesis, not lineage. Our lineage is not the fanout merger; we should not run it (base artifact dir is parent, outside lineage). Skip.\n\nNow the step_update_config_status: marking status complete inside lineage config. Fine.\n\n`phase_save`: not requested. Skip.\n\nAlso `step_hydrate_summary_metrics` is a read step; we execute mentally.\n\n`step_create_registry` content exactly:\n`{\"openQuestions\":[],\"resolvedQuestions\":[],\"keyFindings\":[],\"ruledOutDirections\":[],\"metrics\":{\"iterationsCompleted\":0,\"openQuestions\":0,\"resolvedQuestions\":0,\"keyFindings\":0,\"convergenceScore\":0,\"coverageBySources\":{}}}`\n\nThen reducer will rewrite it.\n\nLet's look at a completed lineage example in the repo's specs to model files. Search for `fanout-live/lineages` in specs."}
